The original does a linear scan over all message profiles on every call. This version builds a lookup table indexed by message number once, on first use, so each later call is a single array read.

- **Thread safety:** the table is a function-local static, so C++ initializes it thread-safely.
- **Not a mutable-state cache:** it is built only from the constant `mesgs` table and is never modified afterwards.
- **Same result for duplicate numbers:** if a message number appears more than once, the table keeps its first index, which is the entry the linear search would have returned.
- **Not-found case:** any number with no matching entry still returns `NULL`.
- **Memory:** the table holds 65536 `FIT_UINT16` entries, about 128 KB.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct LookupTable
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[65536];
        LookupTable()
        {
            for (int i = 0; i < 65536; i++)
                idx[i] = 0xFFFF;
            for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
            {
                FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
                if (idx[n] == 0xFFFF)
                    idx[n] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
            }
        }
    };
    static const LookupTable table;

    const FIT_UINT16 i = table.idx[num];
    if (i == 0xFFFF)
        return NULL;
    return &mesgs[i];
}
```